{
  "dom_id": "step-q71081",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t,\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}⟩$ is differentiable.",
  "worked_solution": "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)}^{′}=\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $t$ is\n\n$$\n{(t)}^{′}=1.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1})}^{′}=-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$",
